# Copy this file to .env at the repo root and fill in the values you need.
# The server loads .env from the repo root on startup (dotenvy::dotenv()
# in src/server/src/main.rs) — `pnpm dev` / `cargo run -p thunderforge` both
# run with the repo root as their working directory, so this is the only
# .env file the backend reads. (Frontend VITE_* variables are a separate,
# Vite-managed concern scoped to apps/web/ — see apps/web/.env* if you need
# to override those; they are not read from this file.)

# --- Required ---

DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:password@localhost/thunderforge

# --- Core server config (all optional — shown with their defaults) ---

# Session/cookie signing secret. The built-in default is a placeholder and
# is NOT safe for anything beyond local dev — set a real one (64+ random
# bytes, base64-encoded) for any shared or persistent environment.
# THUNDERFORGE_SECRET=

# Where the server stores local data (uploaded assets, manifest, etc.) when
# not using RustFS. Defaults to ./data under the current working directory.
# THUNDERFORGE_DATA_PATH=

# Set to 1/true/yes/on to mark session cookies Secure (requires HTTPS).
# Defaults to false, which is correct for local dev over plain HTTP.
# THUNDERFORGE_SECURE_COOKIES=false

# --- RustFS object storage (spec 002 — canvas image assets) ---
# Dev-only root credentials; the server mints short-lived, single-object-
# scoped STS credentials from these per write — never exposed to a client.
# See docs/adrs/20260820-039-rustfs_scoped_asset_storage.md.
# All five have built-in defaults matching the docker-compose rustfs
# service (see compose.yml) — only override if you're pointing at a
# different RustFS/S3-compatible endpoint.

# RUSTFS_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# RUSTFS_REGION=us-east-1
# RUSTFS_BUCKET=thunderforge-canvas-assets
# RUSTFS_ROOT_ACCESS_KEY=thunderforge-rustfs-root
# RUSTFS_ROOT_SECRET_KEY=thunderforge-rustfs-root-secret

# --- OAuth login providers (spec 007, ADR-041) ---
# Optional. Any provider you configure here appears as a login button
# automatically, with no admin-panel step needed — see
# specs/007-configurable-auth-providers/ and ADR-041 for the full design.
#
# Naming scheme: OAUTH_<PROVIDER>_[<INSTANCE>_]<FIELD>
#   <PROVIDER>  a built-in preset name (DISCORD, GITHUB, GOOGLE, KEYCLOAK),
#               or any other name to use the fully generic OAuth2/OIDC
#               template (see the MYSERVICE example below).
#   <INSTANCE>  optional — set to run two instances of the same provider
#               side by side (e.g. two Keycloak realms). Omit for the
#               default/only instance of that provider.
#   <FIELD>     CLIENT_ID | CLIENT_SECRET | LABEL, plus provider-specific
#               fields (ISSUER_URL for Keycloak; AUTHORIZATION_URL /
#               TOKEN_URL / USERINFO_URL / SCOPES for a generic provider).
#
# Every instance also accepts a _LABEL override to replace its default
# button text (e.g. "Log in with <LABEL>" instead of the provider's name).

# Discord (built-in preset — only client id/secret needed):
# OAUTH_DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=

# GitHub (built-in preset):
# OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Google (built-in preset):
# OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Keycloak / self-hosted generic-OIDC (built-in preset — needs an issuer
# URL since, unlike Discord/GitHub/Google, there's no single fixed
# endpoint; auth/token/userinfo URLs are derived from it automatically):
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_ISSUER_URL=https://idp.example.com/realms/myrealm
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_LABEL=

# A second, named Keycloak instance alongside the default one above:
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_WORK_ISSUER_URL=https://work.example.com/realms/myrealm
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_WORK_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_WORK_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OAUTH_KEYCLOAK_WORK_LABEL=Work SSO

# Any other OAuth2/OIDC provider with no built-in preset — supply the full
# endpoint set yourself (scopes are space-separated, USERINFO_URL optional):
# OAUTH_MYSERVICE_CLIENT_ID=
# OAUTH_MYSERVICE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OAUTH_MYSERVICE_AUTHORIZATION_URL=https://myservice.example/oauth/authorize
# OAUTH_MYSERVICE_TOKEN_URL=https://myservice.example/oauth/token
# OAUTH_MYSERVICE_USERINFO_URL=https://myservice.example/oauth/userinfo
# OAUTH_MYSERVICE_SCOPES=openid profile email

# --- Sharing a dev server publicly (make dev-tunnel) -------------------------
# Optional. Without these, `make dev-tunnel` opens a Cloudflare *quick*
# tunnel: no account needed, but the https://<random>.trycloudflare.com URL
# changes every run and the service is rate-limited often enough that "the
# tunnel is broken" is usually that.
#
# Set both for a *named* tunnel with a hostname that survives restarts, so a
# link you send someone keeps working. Create the tunnel in the Cloudflare
# Zero Trust dashboard, point its public hostname at http://localhost:5173,
# and paste its token here.
#
# TUNNEL_HOSTNAME is not optional when TUNNEL_TOKEN is set: Vite's
# server.allowedHosts must name the hostname or it answers 403 to a tunnel
# that is working perfectly.
#
# NOTE: anyone with the URL can reach the dev server, and Vite serves your
# TypeScript source over it. Treat a tunnelled dev server as public.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=
TUNNEL_HOSTNAME=

# --- Lore repository synchronisation (spec 034, optional) --------------------
#
# An instance with none of these set offers nothing to connect: the world's
# settings show no repository card, and the operator diagnostic says which of
# these is missing. That is deliberate — a Game Master must never be shown a
# flow that cannot complete.
#
# Register an application with your repository host, then set:
#
# The application's NUMERIC identifier. Not its slug; they are different, and
# swapping them fails as an authentication error that reads like a bad key.
# SYNC_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
#
# The application's URL SLUG, which the install link is built from.
# SYNC_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
#
# The application's PEM private key. Three variables, highest precedence
# first, all carrying the same key — set exactly one:
#
#   1. A path to the file. Preferred where you can: Docker and systemd deliver
#      secrets as files precisely because environments leak into logs and into
#      child processes.
#      SYNC_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/repo-app.pem
#
#   2. Base64 of the whole PEM. THE .env-SAFE FORM — one line, no newlines to
#      escape, nothing a shell or a dotenv parser will reinterpret. Produce it
#      with:  base64 -w0 app-key.pem
#      SYNC_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64=
#
#   3. The PEM itself, with real newlines or the literal \n escapes that
#      pasting one into this file produces. Forgiving, but the fiddliest.
#      SYNC_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
#
# The key is PARSED at startup, not at first use. A value that is set but is
# not a usable RSA key is reported as unconfigured rather than becoming an
# error the first time someone connects a repository.
#
# `git` must also be on PATH — synchronisation drives it directly. The server
# says so at startup if it is missing, and does not refuse to boot: every other
# part of the product works without it.

# --- Instance settings (spec 040, all optional) ------------------------------
#
# Every setting this instance has can be set here instead of in the
# administration screens, and a value set here WINS. That is one rule for every
# setting (ADR-088, extending ADR-041 from OAuth to the product): the
# environment beats the instance's stored value beats the shipped default, and
# the administration surface shows which of the three a live value came from.
#
# The consequence worth knowing before you set one: a setting fixed here is
# NOT editable from the screens. It is shown as "fixed by <VARIABLE>" rather
# than greyed out for no stated reason, and an attempt to change it is refused
# by name rather than quietly doing nothing.
#
# Nothing here is required to start. A required setting that is unset is a
# readiness gap and a refusal at the point of use — never a failure to boot.
#
# Who runs this instance. Named on the legal pages.
# THUNDERFORGE_OPERATOR_NAME=
# THUNDERFORGE_OPERATOR_CONTACT_EMAIL=
# THUNDERFORGE_OPERATOR_JURISDICTION=
#
# Where a copyright notice is served. Legally distinct from the support
# address, and required before this instance will publish anything beyond a
# world — playing privately needs none of it.
# THUNDERFORGE_NOTICE_CONTACT_NAME=
# THUNDERFORGE_NOTICE_CONTACT_EMAIL=
# THUNDERFORGE_NOTICE_CONTACT_ADDRESS=
#
# The realm manifest's keys, which until now could only be set in the
# administration screens. Setting one here fixes it.
# THUNDERFORGE_REALM_NAME=
# THUNDERFORGE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=
# THUNDERFORGE_WELCOME_MESSAGE=
# THUNDERFORGE_INTERFACE_PACK_ID=
# THUNDERFORGE_ASSET_PACK_ID=
# THUNDERFORGE_DEFAULT_GAME_SYSTEM_ID=
#
# Who may create an account: open, invite_only or closed. Setting it here
# means it can no longer be changed from the screen while people are trying to
# register, which is usually not what you want on a running community.
# THUNDERFORGE_INSTANCE_ACCESS_POLICY=
#
# Mail. Nothing is sent while this is unconfigured; messages wait rather than
# being discarded, and readiness says so.
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_ENABLED=false
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_HOST=
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_PORT=587
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_USERNAME=
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_PASSWORD=
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=
# THUNDERFORGE_SMTP_FROM_NAME=
#
# --- GitHub applications, at two scales (spec 040 US5) -----------------------
#
# Register ONE application and let every subsystem use it, or one per
# subsystem, or a mixture. Three scopes exist today:
#
#   GLOBAL_GITHUB_APP_*     the fallback every subsystem draws on
#   SYNC_GITHUB_APP_*       lore synchronisation (documented in full above)
#   FEEDBACK_GITHUB_APP_*   in-app feedback
#
# Each takes the same three names — _CLIENT_ID, _SLUG and a private key — and
# the private key takes the same three forms, with the same precedence:
#
#   1. ..._PRIVATE_KEY_FILE     a path. HIGHEST precedence, because Docker and
#                               systemd deliver secrets as files. The path is
#                               read on use; the key itself is never copied
#                               into this instance's database.
#   2. ..._PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64   the whole PEM, base64. The .env-safe form:
#                               base64 -w0 app-key.pem
#   3. ..._PRIVATE_KEY          the PEM itself, real newlines or literal \n
#
# RESOLUTION, and the one part of it that surprises people:
#
#   For subsystem S, the first COMPLETE application wins —
#     1. S,      environment     e.g. SYNC_GITHUB_APP_*
#     2. S,      instance        set in the administration screens
#     3. global, environment     GLOBAL_GITHUB_APP_*
#     4. global, instance        set in the administration screens
#
# SCOPE IS THE OUTER AXIS AND SOURCE IS THE INNER ONE. Everywhere else in this
# file the environment beats the instance's stored value; here, a GLOBAL
# application set in the environment does NOT beat a subsystem application
# configured in the screens. Somebody who configured a subsystem application
# meant it, and a broad variable silently overriding it is the surprise this
# whole feature exists to remove. (research.md § R10, ADR-090.)
#
# AN APPLICATION RESOLVES WHOLE. A subsystem application with a client ID and
# no private key does NOT borrow the global one's key. It is reported as
# incomplete, naming what is missing, and the subsystem falls through to the
# global application ENTIRE. Half of one credential and half of another is a
# configuration nobody chose and nobody could debug — it fails as an
# authentication error that reads like a bad key.
#
# Every key is PARSED when it is configured, not at first use, in the
# environment and in the screens alike. A value that is present and is not a
# key is refused rather than stored: that is the case a presence check calls
# "configured".
#
# The administration screen shows, for each scope, where each value came from
# and — wherever global credentials are set — which subsystems the application
# will act for. That list grows as subsystems are added.
#
# GLOBAL_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# GLOBAL_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
# GLOBAL_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=
# GLOBAL_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64=
# GLOBAL_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
#
# FEEDBACK_GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
# FEEDBACK_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
# FEEDBACK_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE=
# FEEDBACK_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_BASE64=
# FEEDBACK_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=
